				"url": "https://defcon.social/@corbin",
				"avatarUrl": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/avatars/original/missing.png"
			},
			"date": "2023-08-22T03:05:34.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://indieweb.social/@brianleroux\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>brianleroux</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fosstodon.org/@boneskull\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>boneskull</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> This is my favorite takeaway. The reason to target JS was that it was \"close to the metal\" in terms of portable Web media; the entire cottage industry of \"transpiler to JS\" is built upon that requirement. I can't help but speculate that WASM is part of the picture; the overwhelming complexity had to collapse somehow, and shifting foundations would be a reasonable cause.</p><p>There was discussion about this recently, on Lobsters: <a href=\"https://lobste.rs/s/id9jet/transpiler_meaningless_word\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">lobste.rs/s/id9jet/transpiler_</span><span class=\"invisible\">meaningless_word</span></a></p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
